{% set local_problem %}
<p>Decatur's river location creates gutter challenges you won't find 20 miles inland. Tennessee River wraps around your city. Wheeler Lake sits east. Constant humidity hangs in the air. Perfect recipe for gutter problems — and the consequences of neglect hit harder here than anywhere else we serve.</p>

<p>Start with debris. Yes, local trees drop leaves and needles like everywhere else. But Decatur also gets wind-blown organics from the river system — leaves, seed pods, pollen, fine particulates traveling miles on the breeze. Even homes with minimal tree coverage find their gutters filling with debris from elsewhere.</p>

<p>Then there's the humidity factor. In most of North Alabama, leaves in gutters dry out and can be scooped easily. In Decatur's river air, those leaves decompose into thick composted sludge within 3-4 weeks. That muck is harder to remove, holds moisture against your gutters and fascia, and creates perfect conditions for mosquitoes, mold, and corrosion.</p>

<p>The consequences are immediate. Overflow during Decatur's intense storms sends water right next to your foundation — which is already dealing with groundwater pressure from a naturally high water table (often just 3-4 feet below foundation level during wet seasons). You're adding water to soil that's already saturated. Foundation damage, basement moisture, and landscape erosion follow.</p>

<p><strong>The math:</strong> Foundation repair in Decatur runs $10,000-30,000. Annual gutter cleaning costs $400-600 total. We've seen homeowners skip cleanings to save money, then spend five figures fixing foundation cracks. Don't learn this lesson the expensive way.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Our gutter cleaning is comprehensive — and designed for Decatur's specific conditions.</p>

<p><strong>Step 1: Hand removal.</strong> We pull all debris from gutter troughs by hand — leaves, needles, river-blown organics, decomposed sludge, roof grit. We don't blow debris onto your lawn or push it toward downspouts. We bag it and haul it away.</p>

<p><strong>Step 2: Sludge removal.</strong> In Decatur's humidity, there's always a layer of decomposed organic matter at the bottom of gutters. Most DIY cleanings miss this. We scrape it out completely — that's the stuff holding moisture against your gutter and accelerating corrosion.</p>

<p><strong>Step 3: Downspout flush.</strong> Every downspout gets flushed with water until it runs clear. Downspout clogs are the #1 thing homeowners miss when cleaning their own gutters. In Decatur, wet debris compacts into solid plugs that partially block flow — and you can't see them from above.</p>

<p><strong>Step 4: Inspection.</strong> While we're up there, we check for: rust at seams, loose or missing hangers, fascia showing early rot signs, gaps that let water behind the gutters, and any humidity damage specific to river-town conditions. We document problems and let you know what we find.</p>

<p><strong>Step 5: Cleanup.</strong> When we're done, your property looks better than when we arrived. No debris on the lawn, no mess on the driveway.</p>

<p><strong>What we won't do:</strong> Tell you everything is "fine" when it's not. We see problems constantly that previous cleaners ignored. You'll get an honest assessment of what's actually going on up there.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set why_us %}
<p>We've cleaned gutters across <a href="/recent-projects/">Morgan County</a> for over 20 years — long enough to understand what the Tennessee River does to homes. Decatur isn't the same as Huntsville or Madison. Higher humidity, different debris, more severe consequences when gutters fail.</p>

<p>We bring the right equipment, the experience to work safely and efficiently, and local knowledge that matters. When you call Blue River Gutters, you're getting professionals who know your city.</p>

<p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> If you hire someone for gutter cleaning and they don't flush your downspouts, you're not getting a complete service. Debris compacts into partial clogs that restrict flow — especially in Decatur where everything stays wet. Ask specifically. Some companies skip this step because it takes extra time. We don't.</p>

<p><strong>Our recommendation for Decatur:</strong> Schedule cleanings in late November (after leaves drop), late April (after spring pollen/seeds), and late August if you have significant pine or river debris exposure. Three cleanings a year keeps everything flowing in this climate. Consider gutter guards if you're tired of the ongoing expense.</p>
{% endset %}
